      "id": "fbgrxu5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning and Importance of DPSP"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Directive Principles of State Policy (DPSP) are enumerated in Part IV, Articles 36 to 51 of the Indian Constitution.",
        "The concept was borrowed from the Irish Constitution of 1937, which had copied it from the Spanish Constitution.",
        "DPSPs are non-justiciable in nature, meaning they are not legally enforceable by courts for their violation.",
        "They seek to establish economic and social democracy and realize the goal of a welfare state.",
        "DPSPs are broadly classified into Socialist, Gandhian, and Liberal–Intellectual principles based on their content and direction."
      ],
      "id": "1ju2j5z"
    },
